research is needed before the subagent call. The DeepInvestigator should receive the task instructions as context for UI/UX alignment with the target platform, considering features such as website building capabilities.\n\n\nThe system's core should replicate the target platform's virtual machine farm capabilities, especially the integrated browser functionality. A third-party online browser solution (e.g., Firecrawl), with API key support, will replace running a dedicated virtual machine. Speech-to-text functionality will be integrated, leveraging an API.\n\nThe design should reflect the target platform's aesthetics and interaction patterns, including proactive tags and verb-based button labels. Key aspects include how approvals and widgets function. Further investigation is needed to align the project with these new requirements, considering account support and other platform integrations.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"invoke_subagent","args":{"Model":"inherit","Prompt":"**Task**: The issue now is that this project is designed to be an open-source HARK Pro, and that is just it. The nature of it is that potentially we could try to modify our architecture to meet theirs, so we can just try to adapt it. So the good on our end, we can try to keep. The bad, we can try to adjust, but I think I like their design philosophy a lot, so I want it to mirror that design philosophy. So for now, we'll just try to let it run in a sandbox, but the primary features that their AI model has includes things like the ability to actually build small websites and showcase it to the users, which our sandbox does not have. So our sandbox should actually mirror their VM farm, if you get where I'm coming from. So it should be able to mirror the core functionalities, including the browser. So for the browser, we'll make use of a third-party online browser, something like the Firecrawl browser or something like that, so we'll have proper support for the API keys as opposed to running a dedicated virtual machine. Voice, we will make use of an STT model, probably something using the GROQ API for the voice, that being the speech-to-text. The companion mascot can be removed for now. It isn't something that is very required, this is the core necessity, but their general design language and aesthetic is something that should be mirrored, as it is one of the core functionalities of their platform, something that people genuinely like. So the way they go about approvals, the way they go about even the widgets, the way they go about things like the proactivity tags. For example, you can see a tag like Michael wants to book an email, then you see a button there that says Draft, indicating that it's going to draft the email. So the buttons there are actually verbs, if you get where I'm coming from. So the whole design, I need you to conduct deeper research and update your findings in order to suit the new requirements of the project. Also consider things like the account support, the MCP connections, and so on.\n\n**Additional Context**:\nIn the previous turn, an initial UI/UX comparative investigation between Hark Pro and Vigil was conducted, analyzing how each platform responds to tasks, their proactivity, information architecture, communication tone, companion mascot, and codebase verification. The user is now steering the project to mirror Hark Pro's design philosophy and architecture under new specifications (sandboxed micro-app preview/hosting, third-party browser integration like Firecrawl/browser APIs with API key support instead of dedicated local VM farm, Groq STT for voice, removing the companion mascot, Hark's verb-based action buttons/proactivity tags, approvals, widgets, account support, and MCP connections).","Role":"Deep UX Investigator","TypeName":"DeepInvestigator","Workspace":"inherit"}}]}
