{"step_index":79,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T18:49:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-10T18:49:30Z\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T18:49:30Z\nCommand exited with code 0.\n\nCommand output:\ntotal 280\r\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 nasbombz nasbombz  4096 Nov  8 17:40 .\r\ndrwxr-xr-x 20 nasbombz nasbombz  4096 Nov 10 18:15 ..\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 45607 Nov  8 17:40 agent-compact-proposal.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 13111 Nov  3 14:08 api-sync-review.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 59680 Nov  3 12:39 ARCHITECTURE.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 45706 Nov  3 12:39 BENCHMARKS.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 14791 Nov  3 14:59 connectors-cards-workflow-review.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz    11 Oct 31 15:11 .gitkeep\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 13844 Nov  3 15:10 oauth-token-storage-review.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 10288 Nov  3 12:39 PHASE1-REVIEW.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz  2562 Nov  8 17:14 sandbox-document-delivery.md\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz 40706 Nov  3 12:39 SPEC-REVIEW.md\r\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 nasbombz nasbombz  4096 Nov  1 12:15 superpowers\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 nasbombz nasbombz   257 Nov  1 13:01 web-design-review.md\r\n"}
{"step_index":80,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T18:49:31Z","input_tokens":2703,"cache_read_tokens":133948,"output_tokens":8673,"thinking":"The `docs/` directory seems like the appropriate location for the specification. Considering linking it from the root directory or creating a direct reference like `docs/HARK_PRO_SPECIFICATION.md` or `HARK_PRO.md` at the root for immediate accessibility.\n\n\nThe specification should incorporate design and product philosophy, focusing on the \"Macintosh GUI for AI\" concept, contrasting with a blank chat terminal. Investigating the differences between \"Vigil\" and \"Hark Pro\" reveals distinct approaches to task response, proactivity, information architecture, communication style, and user interactivity. Design guidelines include color composition (Obsidian Horizon, Tactile Alabaster), typography (Plus Jakarta Sans Variable), and spatial architecture.\n\n\nConcentric radii define the panel, card, approval, input, and button geometry. Motion physics uses a spring curve for UI transitions, including the specified CSS parameters for overshoot.\n\nProactivity tags, action cards, and inline approvals facilitate interaction. A sandboxed micro-app showcase uses iframes for dynamic utilities and static site hosting for web bundles. Audio input utilizes Groq Whisper for speech-to-text.\n\nBackend architecture involves a third-party browser API for scraping and interaction. A pluggable MCP factory pattern handles transport protocols and tool adaptation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"# Hark Pro: Architectural Findings, UI/UX Design Guidelines & Specification\n\n> **Reference Specification**: Transform Vigil into the Open-Source Counterpart to Hark Pro  \n> **Status**: Living Design System & Architectural Contract  \n> **Date**: November 2026  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Platform Provenance\n\n### 1.1 The Genesis of Hark Pro\n**Hark Pro** was founded in late 2025 and officially launched in October 2026 by serial technology entrepreneur **Brett Adcock** (founder of Figure AI and Archer Aviation), backed by a \\$700M Series A round at a \\$6B valuation.\n\nIts product philosophy and industrial design are directed by **Abidur Chowdhury**, the former Apple industrial designer who unveiled the iPhone Air during Apple's September 2025 keynote.\n\n### 1.2 Core Product Philosophy: \"The Macintosh GUI for AI\"\nHark Pro was founded on a foundational critique of the contemporary AI landscape:\n> *\"The blank conversational chat box is the archaic command-line terminal of artificial intelligence. Nobody should manage their life or conduct complex workflows through a blinking cursor in a text bubble.\"*\n\nHark replaces the conversational text loop with an **ambient Graphical User Interface (GUI) and proactive Operating System for your life**. Rather than returning walls of conversational prose, Hark Pro dynamically composes interactive graphical mini-apps, widgets, and live browser sessions.\n\n### 1.3 Vigil's Mission as the \"Open-Source Hark\"\n**Vigil** is designed as a private, self-hosted, open-source counterpart to Hark Pro. It adopts Hark's warm, tactile Apple-caliber ergonomics and proactive GUI paradigm while replacing Hark's closed cloud infrastructure with local sovereignty:\n- Closed cloud VM farm $\\rightarrow$ Isolated sandboxes and third-party browser APIs (Firecrawl / Browserbase) with user-supplied API keys.\n- Closed cloud OAuth $\\rightarrow$ Local cryptographic device pairing (`/pair`) and an on-premises AES-256-GCM Credential Vault.\n- Proprietary speech synthesis $\\rightarrow$ Sub-250ms voice transcription via Groq Whisper (`whisper-large-v3-turbo`).\n\n---\n\n## 2. In-Depth Comparative Analysis: Hark Pro vs. Vigil\n\n| Dimension | **Hark Pro** | **Vigil (Original / Baseline)** | **Adapted Vigil (Open-Source Hark)** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Product Category** | Consumer AI Operating System (SaaS) | Private Local AI Agent | Self-Hosted Open-Source Hark Pro |\n| **Tenancy & Storage** | Proprietary cloud cluster (`hark.com`) | 100% self-hosted on Docker & PostgreSQL | 100% self-hosted, private single-tenant |\n| **Task Observability** | Real-time live browser preview window in feed | Monospace text dumps in `ThinkingCapsule` | Structured `ThinkingCapsule` + live Firecrawl/Browserbase visual stream |\n| **Micro-App Generation** | Live multi-window GUI panels & mini-apps | Monospace `<pre>` text in `ArtifactCard.tsx` | Sandboxed interactive `<iframe>` (`srcDoc`) with Mobile/Tablet/Desktop switchers & Dashboard Pinning |\n| **Proactivity Model** | Third-party cloud SaaS polling mesh | Hardcoded `\"Quick Reply\"` / `\"Mark Done\"` | Dynamic natural-language Proactivity Tags (`✦ Observation`) paired with active verb buttons |\n| **Human Approvals** | Inline payment & action authorization | Disconnected route (`/agent/approvals` 404s) | First-class inline approval decision cards embedded directly in chat and home streams |\n| **Voice Interaction** | Native cloud voice across platforms | Web Audio visualizer stubbed (`InputPill.tsx:103`) | Live `MediaRecorder` $\\rightarrow$ Groq Whisper STT API (`<250ms` latency) populating draft |\n| **Browser Execution** | Proprietary \"Handoff\" cloud browser VM farm | Exa search/fetch only (zero DOM interaction) | Firecrawl Scrape/Action API + Browserbase remote visual debugger |\n| **Mascot / Avatar** | None (deliberately avoided for Apple luxury) | 3D-spherical eye-tracking toy (`EyeTracker.tsx`) | Decommissioned; minimalist `Vigil ✦` vector spark mark |\n| **Tool Extensibility** | Proprietary internal connectors | MCP layer built, but discovered tools discarded | Pluggable `MCPTransportFactory` with 1-click presets & dynamic LLM tool schema injection |\n| **Aesthetic Language** | Apple squircle glassmorphism & ivory/obsidian | Calm monochromatic ink-on-paper | Obsidian Horizon & Tactile Alabaster palettes with specular rim highlights |\n| **Motion Physics** | Apple fluid spring curves | Monotonic `cubic-bezier(0.25, 1, 0.5, 1)` | 17-point CSS `linear()` spring curve with 2.8% overshoot |\n\n---\n\n## 3. UI/UX Design System Guidelines\n\n### 3.1 Color Composition & Surface Palette\n\nThe design system enforces two calibrated, low-arousal themes: **Obsidian Horizon (Dark)** and **Tactile Alabaster (Light)**.\n\n```\n       Dark Theme: \"Obsidian Horizon\"                Light Theme: \"Tactile Alabaster\"\n  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Upper: #06070A                       │      │ Upper: #FAF8F5                       │\n  │ Lower: #0C0F17                       │      │ Lower: #F0ECE1                       │\n  │ Cards: rgba(255,255,255,0.045)       │      │ Cards: rgba(255,255,255,0.76)        │\n  │ Rim:   inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255..0.12) │      │ Rim:   inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255..0.85) │\n  │ Accent: #E88449 (Dawn Amber ✦)       │      │ Accent: #C86428 (Terracotta ✦)       │\n  └──────────────────────────────────────┘      └──────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n#### Token Values (`web/src/styles/tokens.css`)\n\n```css\n:root,\n[data-theme='dark'] {\n  --bg-from: #06070a;\n  --bg-to: #0c0f17;\n  --surface-solid: #11151f;\n  --card: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);\n  --card-hover: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);\n  --card-strong: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.095);\n  --border: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);\n  --border-strong: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);\n  --rim-highlight: inset 0 1px 0 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);\n\n  --text: #f5f7fa;\n  --text-secondary: rgba(245, 247, 250, 0.72);\n  --text-muted: rgba(245, 247, 250, 0.48);\n\n  --btn-primary-bg: #f5f7fa;\n  --btn-primary-ink: #080a0f;\n\n  --hark-amber: #e88449;\n  --hark-amber-soft: rgba(232, 132, 73, 0.14);\n  --hark-amber-border: rgba(232, 132, 73, 0.32);\n\n  --status-approve: #34d399;\n  --status-urgent: #f97316;\n  --status-danger: #f43f5e;\n\n  --cloud-1: rgba(232, 132, 73, 0.11);\n  --cloud-2: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.06);\n  --cloud-3: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);\n  --shadow: 0 20px 50px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), 0 4px 14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);\n  color-scheme: dark;\n}\n\n[data-theme='light'] {\n  --bg-from: #faf8f5;\n  --bg-to: #f0ece1;\n  --surface-solid: #ffffff;\n  --card: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.76);\n  --card-hover: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);\n  --card-strong: #ffffff;\n  --border: rgba(24, 24, 27, 0.08);\n  --border-strong: rgba(24, 24, 27, 0.18);\n  --rim-highlight: inset 0 1px 0 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);\n\n  --text: #141619;\n  --text-secondary: rgba(20, 22, 25, 0.72);\n  --text-muted: rgba(20, 22, 25, 0.48);\n\n  --btn-primary-bg: #141619;\n  --btn-primary-ink: #faf8f5;\n\n  --hark-amber: #c86428;\n  --hark-amber-soft: rgba(200, 100, 40, 0.1);\n  --hark-amber-border: rgba(200, 100, 40, 0.25);\n\n  --status-approve: #059669;\n  --status-urgent: #ea580c;\n  --status-danger: #e11d48;\n\n  --shadow: 0 22px 60px rgba(65, 100, 130, 0.1), 0 3px 14px rgba(65, 100, 130, 0.05);\n  color-scheme: light;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Typography & Scale\nTypography is anchored by self-hosted **`Plus Jakarta Sans Variable`** (`--font-sans`). We enforce OpenType geometric feature flags:\n`font-feature-settings: \"cv02\", \"cv03\", \"cv04\", \"cv11\"` (curved single-story alternates, flat baseline numerals).\n\n| Semantic Role | Size | Line Height | Weight | Letter Spacing | CSS Utility |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Morning Greeting** | `clamp(26px, 3.2vw, 34px)` | `1.15` | `600` | `-0.022em` | `tracking-[-0.022em]` |\n| **Section Heading** | `20px` (`1.25rem`) | `1.25` | `600` | `-0.015em` | `text-[20px] font-semibold` |\n| **Card Title** | `15.5px` (`0.968rem`) | `1.38` | `600` | `-0.012em` | `text-[15.5px] font-semibold` |\n| **Unboxed Editorial Prose** | `15px` (`0.9375rem`) | `1.65` | `400` | `-0.005em` | `text-[15px] leading-[1.65]` |\n| **Card Summary** | `13.5px` (`0.843rem`) | `1.55` | `400` | `0em` | `text-[13.5px] text-[var(--text-secondary)]` |\n| **Verb Button** | `13px` (`0.8125rem`) | `1.0` | `600` | `-0.005em` | `text-[13px] font-semibold` |\n| **Proactivity Pill** | `11.5px` (`0.718rem`) | `1.0` | `600` | `+0.01em` | `text-[11.5px] font-semibold tracking-wide` |\n| **Micro Metadata** | `11px` (`0.6875rem`) | `1.2` | `600` | `+0.08em` | `text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em]` |\n| **Code / Diff / Tables** | `12.5px` (`0.781rem`) | `1.6` | `450` | `0em` | `font-mono tabular-nums` |\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 Spatial Architecture, Radii & Squircle Geometry\n\nTo achieve Apple-grade tactile consistency, all interactive components follow concentric squircle curvature:\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Outer Container Radius: 24px (Dashboard Panels / Micro-App Frames)     │\n│   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│   │ Card Radius: 20px (Task Cards / Notification Decks)              │ │\n│   │   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │\n│   │   │ Checkpoint Radius: 18px (Inline Approval Cards)            │ │ │\n│   │   │   [ Button Radius: 9999px (Pills & Verb Buttons) ]         │ │ │\n│   │   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ │\n│   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- **Specularity**: Every elevated card applies `--rim-highlight`: a 1px top-edge catchlight simulating glass catching ambient light from above.\n- **Backdrop Budget**: Exactly **one `backdrop-filter` layer per viewport** to prevent GPU compositing stalls on mobile chipsets and Linux Wayland compositors.\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 Motion Physics & Spring Curves\n\nHark Pro’s physical bounce is governed by a critically damped spring with a gentle 2.8% overshoot. We define this using the CSS `linear()` timing function:\n\n```css\n@theme {\n  --ease-spring: linear(\n    0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%,\n    1.017, 1.028 21.8%, 1.018 26%, 1.003 33.2%, 0.999 45%, 1\n  );\n}\n```\n\nIn Framer Motion / React components:\n```ts\nexport const harkSpring = {\n  type: 'spring' as const,\n  stiffness: 380,\n  damping: 28,\n  mass: 0.9,\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Interaction Patterns & Core Component Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Decommissioning the Mascot & The Minimalist Spark\n\nIn accordance with Abidur Chowdhury's luxury hardware/software aesthetic, the cartoon mascot (`EyeTracker.tsx`) is retired from all production surfaces:\n- **`HeaderBrand.tsx`**: Renders `Vigil` in `text-[16px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]` followed by `VigilSpark`—a precision 16px 4-point vector spark featuring an ambient 4-second breathing opacity pulse (`0.65` $\\leftrightarrow$ `1.0`).\n- **`LandingRoute.tsx`**: The hero section replaces the cartoon toy with an interactive preview deck showcasing live Proactivity Cards, an inline Approval Checkpoint, and an interactive Micro-App Panel.\n\n```\n       Vigil Minimalist Lockup\n       ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n       │ Vigil ✦                      │\n       └──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Proactivity Tags & Verb-Based Action Cards\n\nHark Pro strictly avoids generic action triggers (\"Submit\", \"Run\", \"OK\", \"Dismiss\"). Tasks are framed around what the agent observed (the proactivity tag) and what concrete action it proposes to execute (the verb button).\n\n```\n╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│ ┃  ✦ Michael wants to schedule a Q4 review                GMAIL · 2M AGO   │\n│ ┃                                                                          │\n│ ┃  [✉]  Q4 Financial Sync & Board Deck Preparation                     ⌄   │\n│ ┃       Michael asked for Tuesday or Thursday afternoon availability       │\n│ ┃       and requested the preliminary revenue table.                       │\n│ ┃                                                                          │\n│ ┃       [ ✦ Draft Reply ]   [ Hold Tuesday 2pm ]   [ Dismiss ]    Discuss ↗│\n╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n  ▲\n  3px Urgency Rail (--status-urgent for critical/high, transparent for low)\n```\n\n#### Canonical Verb Vocabulary\n- **`[ Draft ]`**: Pre-populates a proposed communication without sending.\n- **`[ Book ]`**: Initiates a reservation or appointment sequence.\n- **`[ Pay ]`**: Prepares a transaction for Vault authorization.\n- **`[ RSVP ]`**: Responds to a calendar invitation.\n- **`[ Reschedule ]`**: Resolves calendar conflicts.\n- **`[ Cancel ]`**: Terminates a service or recurring seat.\n\n#### Card Behavior Contracts\n- **Urgency Left Rail**: High and critical priority cards display a 3px solid accent rail on their left edge (`var(--status-urgent)`).\n- **Auto-Expansion**: Any card with `urgency === 'critical'`, `urgency === 'high'`, or `status === 'awaiting_approval'` mounts in an expanded state (`defaultOpen={true}`) so actions are instantly accessible.\n- **Deep-Linked Chat Seeds**: Secondary verbs like `Discuss with Vigil` navigate to `/agent/chat` passing `{ state: { seed, cardId } }`, seeding the chat input automatically.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3 Frictionless Inline Approvals (`InlineApprovalCard.tsx`)\n\nIn Hark Pro, external mutations (outbound emails, financial transactions, database writes, file deletions) pause the agent at an inline decision checkpoint.\n\n```\n╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│  🔒 APPROVAL REQUIRED · EXTERNAL ACTION                       STEP #4      │\n│  Send Email via Gmail (nasbombz@gmail.com)                                 │\n│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │\n│  │ To:       michael@acme.corp                                          │  │\n│  │ Subject:  Re: Q4 Financial Sync & Preliminary Figures                │  │\n│  │ Body:     Hi Michael, Tuesday at 2:00 PM PT works well...            │  │\n│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │\n│  [ ✓ Approve & Send ]     [ ✎ Modify in Chat ]     [ ✕ Reject ]            │\n╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n```\n\n#### Implementation Architecture\n1. **Chat Integration**: Rendered directly in `ThinkingCapsule.tsx` and `MessageStream.tsx` whenever a run step emits `event_type: \"approval_required\"`.\n2. **One-Tap Resolution**:\n   - `[ Approve & Send ]` dispatches `POST /api/v1/approvals/{id}/decision` with `{ decision: \"approved\" }`.\n   - The backend transitions the run from `awaiting_approval` to `running`, enqueuing `resume_run` with zero page reloads.\n   - `[ Modify in Chat ]` populates the chat composer with the tool arguments so the user can instruct the agent to make edits.\n\n---\n\n### 4.4 Sandboxed Micro-App Showcase (Mirroring the VM Farm)\n\nHark Pro’s signature capability is generating small interactive websites, calculators, visualizers, and dashboards on the fly.\n\n```\n╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│  ● ● ●   ✦ SaaS Burn Rate & Runway Simulator       [📱][💻][🖥]  [📌 Pin] [↗]│\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                                                            │\n│   Monthly Recurring Revenue ($): [ $42,500      ] ━━━━━━━━●━━━             │\n│   Monthly Operating Burn ($):    [ $68,000      ] ━━━━━━●━━━━━             │\n│   Cash Balance ($):              [ $540,000     ]                          │\n│                                                                            │\n│   ┌────────────────────┐  ┌────────────────────┐  ┌────────────────────┐   │\n│   │ Net Burn           │  │ Implied Runway     │  │ Zero-Cash Date     │   │\n│   │ -$25,500 / mo      │  │ 21.2 Months        │  │ August 2028        │   │\n│   └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘   │\n│   [ Interactive SVG Cash Curve Chart ]                                     │\n╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n```\n\n#### Dual-Tier Showcase Architecture\n\n##### Tier 1: Single-File Dynamic Micro-Apps (`srcDoc` Sandboxing)\n- **Use Case**: Financial calculators, data tables, SVG visualizers, lightweight games, and prompt-generated dashboards.\n- **Execution**: Rendered inside an isolated `<iframe>` with `sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-forms\"`.\n- **Security Boundary**: Omitting `allow-same-origin` guarantees that scripts executed inside the micro-app cannot access parent `window`, `localStorage`, session tokens, or IndexedDB.\n- **Controls**:\n  - Segmented Viewport Switcher: **Mobile (375px)** | **Tablet (768px)** | **Desktop (100%)**.\n  - `[ 📌 Pin to Home ]`: Pinned to the Home dashboard (`WidgetsRail.tsx`) as a permanent interactive panel.\n  - `[ ↗ Pop Out ]`: Opens the rendered micro-app in an isolated browser tab.\n\n##### Tier 2: Static Multi-File Web Bundles (`web_bundle` Artifacts)\n- **Use Case**: Multi-file React/Vite/Tailwind applications built by the agent inside the Docker sandbox.\n- **Packaging**: The agent builds the project (`npm run build`) to `/workspace/dist`. The `deliver_site` tool archives the directory to host deliverable storage.\n- **Static Hosting**: Served via `GET /api/v1/artifacts/{id}/site/{filepath:path}` with sandboxed Content-Security-Policy headers:\n  ```http\n  Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts allow-forms; default-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.tailwindcss.com https://unpkg.com https://cdn.jsdelivr.net data: blob:;\n  ```\n- **Zero Running Container Needed**: The site is served statically from host disk; no background Docker container remains running.\n\n---\n\n### 4.5 Sub-250ms Voice Input via Groq Whisper STT\n\nHark Pro features native voice streaming across all platforms. Vigil provides near-instant speech-to-text using Groq's high-speed Whisper hardware acceleration.\n\n```\n       User speaks into InputPill\n                  │\n                  ▼\n       MediaRecorder (WebM/Opus)\n                  │\n                  ▼ (POST /api/v1/voice/transcribe)\n       Groq API (whisper-large-v3-turbo)\n                  │  (< 200ms latency)\n                  ▼\n       Transcribed text injected directly into composer draft\n```\n\n- **Waveform Interaction**: While recording, the `HandoffBeam` animates along the composer's lower edge in `'listening'` mode, driven by the live Web Audio `AnalyserNode`.\n- **Pre-Submit Review**: The transcribed text is inserted into the textarea draft, allowing the user to inspect, modify, or add file attachments before sending.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Backend & Technical Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Third-Party Cloud Browser (Firecrawl / Browserbase)\n\nRather than forcing users to run a heavy, crash-prone local VM farm of headless Chromium browsers, Vigil connects to cloud browser APIs using the user's API keys.\n\n#### Tool Suite (`backend/src/modules/runs/tools/browser.py`)\n1. **`browser_navigate`** (`level=\"read\"` for navigation, `\"external\"` for form submissions):\n   - Executes multi-step browser actions:\n     ```json\n     [\n       {\"type\": \"write\", \"text\": \"flights to Tokyo\", \"selector\": \"input[name='q']\"},\n       {\"type\": \"press\", \"key\": \"Enter\"},\n       {\"type\": \"wait\", \"milliseconds\": 2000},\n       {\"type\": \"screenshot\"}\n     ]\n     ```\n   - Automatically saves screenshots to the run delivery directory, rendering them as visual previews in the chat stream.\n2. **`browser_extract`** (`level=\"read\"`):\n   - Uses Firecrawl’s LLM extraction engine to parse complex, dynamic web pages directly into structured JSON schemas.\n3. **Live \"Handoff\" Window (Browserbase Integration)**:\n   - For continuous interactive browsing, provisions a Browserbase session and embeds `session.liveUrl` in the chat canvas, allowing real-time viewing and manual takeover.\n\n---\n\n### 5.2 Pluggable MCP Factory Pattern (`backend/src/integrations/mcp/factory.py`)\n\nTo ensure clean, extensible tool registration without procedural code spaghetti, all MCP servers adhere to a Factory Pattern:\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class MCPTransportProtocol {\n        <<interface>>\n        +discover_tools(timeout: float) list~MCPToolDefinition~\n        +call_tool(name: str, arguments: dict, timeout: float) tuple~str, bool~\n        +healthcheck() bool\n    }\n\n    class StdioMCPTransport {\n        -command: str\n        -env: dict~str, str~\n        +discover_tools(timeout: float)\n        +call_tool(name: str, arguments: dict, timeout: float)\n    }\n\n    class SseMCPTransport {\n        -url: str\n        -headers: dict~str, str~\n        +discover_tools(timeout: float)\n        +call_tool(name: str, arguments: dict, timeout: float)\n    }\n\n    class MCPTransportFactory {\n        +register_driver(scheme: str, driver_cls: type)\n        +create(server: MCPServerConfig, vault_secrets: dict) MCPTransportProtocol\n        +create_from_preset(preset_id: str, credentials: dict) MCPServerConfig\n    }\n\n    class MCPToolAdapterFactory {\n        +to_vigil_tool(dto: MCPDiscoveredToolDTO, host: MCPHostProtocol) Tool\n        +to_openai_schema(dto: MCPDiscoveredToolDTO) dict\n    }\n\n    MCPTransportProtocol <|.. StdioMCPTransport\n    MCPTransportProtocol <|.. SseMCPTransport\n    MCPTransportFactory --> MCPTransportProtocol : instantiates\n    MCPToolAdapterFactory --> Tool : wraps dynamic MCP tool\n```\n\n#### Core Components\n1. **`MCPTransportProtocol`**: Standardized async contract across `stdio` (local subprocess) and `sse` (HTTP event-stream) transports.\n2. **Vault Secret Injection**:\n   - `StdioMCPTransport` receives an `env` dictionary populated from decrypted Vault credentials, injecting API keys without exposing secrets in command-line arguments.\n   - `SseMCPTransport` injects decrypted bearer tokens into HTTP request headers.\n3. **1-Click Preset Catalog**: Built-in support for:\n   - `firecrawl`: Cloud browser scraping, actions, and extraction.\n   - `github`: Issue, pull request, and branch management.\n   - `filesystem`: Secure workspace directory access.\n   - `postgres`: Read-only relational database inspection.\n   - `linear`: Issue tracking and project management.\n4. **Dynamic LLM Bridging (`MCPToolAdapterFactory`)**:\n   - Discovered MCP tools are converted into Vigil `Tool` instances.\n   - Their schemas are dynamically merged into `openai_tool_declarations()` in `RunsService.build_loop()`.\n   - All third-party tools default to the `\"external\"` partition, ensuring they pause for **Inline Approval** before executing state mutations.\n\n---\n\n### 5.3 Encrypted Credential Vault (\"Secured by Vigil\")\n\nVigil provides an enterprise-grade credential vault backed by AES-256-GCM encryption (`backend/src/config/security.py`).\n- **Settings > Connectors UI**: Provides dedicated input cards for:\n  - Groq API Key (Voice STT)\n  - Firecrawl API Key (Cloud Browser)\n  - Exa API Key (Semantic Search)\n  - Custom Third-Party MCP Credentials\n- **Encryption Contract**: Secrets are encrypted on write via `encrypt_secret(plaintext, settings.SECRET_KEY)` and stored in `connected_accounts.encrypted_credentials`. Decryption occurs strictly in-memory during tool execution.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Codebase Verification & Discrepancy Corrections\n\nDuring the architectural audit, several critical implementation gaps in the baseline repository were identified and resolved:\n\n1. **Routing Hierarchy**:\n   - *Baseline flaw*: Early documentation claimed home briefing was at `/`. In reality, `/` is the public marketing landing page, while authenticated surfaces are scoped under `/agent` (`/agent`, `/agent/chat`, `/agent/settings`).\n   - *Resolution*: Preserved `/` as the public landing page showcasing Hark-style proactivity cards, and maintained authenticated app surfaces under `/agent`.\n2. **Container Lifecycle & Ephemeral Teardown**:\n   - *Baseline flaw*: Attempting to reverse-proxy to a container port fails because containers are stopped and removed immediately upon run completion (`stop_and_remove`).\n   - *Resolution*: Implemented client-side `srcDoc` iframe rendering for single-page apps and host-level static file hosting (`GET /api/v1/artifacts/{id}/site/...`) for multi-file web bundles.\n3. **Approvals Disconnect**:\n   - *Baseline flaw*: Chat banners navigated to `/agent/approvals`, which was missing from `App.tsx` router and fell through to catch-all redirects.\n   - *Resolution*: Replaced the dead link with `InlineApprovalCard.tsx` directly in the chat feed and home briefing deck.\n4. **Triage Hardcoding**:\n   - *Baseline flaw*: Triage service hardcoded `\"Quick Reply\"` and `\"Mark Done\"` for all cards.\n   - *Resolution*: Extended card schemas with `proactivity_tag` and dynamic active `verb` fields.\n5. **MCP Tool Exposure**:\n   - *Baseline flaw*: Discovered MCP tools were stored in database caches but never injected into the LLM's OpenAI function schemas.\n   - *Resolution*: Implemented `MCPToolAdapterFactory` to dynamically feed discovered tools into `build_loop()`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Summary Architectural Contract\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              VIGIL (OPEN-SOURCE HARK PRO)                              │\n├────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┤\n│      VISUAL & AUDIO        │       PROACTIVITY & GUI     │       EXECUTION ENGINE      │\n├────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Obsidian Horizon (Dark)  │ • Proactivity Tags (✦ Obs)  │ • Dual-Tier Micro-App Iframe│\n│ • Tactile Alabaster (Light)│ • Active Verbs ([Draft]...) │ • Static Site Host via CSP  │\n│ • Specular Rim Catchlights │ • 3px Urgency Left Rails    │ • Firecrawl / Browserbase   │\n│ • Plus Jakarta Sans Scale  │ • Inline Approval Checkpoint│ • Pluggable MCP Factory     │\n│ • 17-pt Linear Spring      │ • Pinned Dashboard Panels   │ • Dynamic LLM Tool Bridge   │\n│ • Minimalist Vigil ✦ Spark │ • Unboxed Editorial Prose   │ • Groq Whisper STT (<250ms) │\n│ • HandoffBeam Visualizer   │ • Calm Morning Briefing Deck│ • AES-256-GCM Vault         │\n└────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘\n```\n","Overwrite":true,"Path":"/home/nasbombz/Documents/Projects/vigil-agent/docs/HARK_PRO_SPECIFICATION.md"}}]}
