{"step_index":327,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:11:31Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T10:11:31Z\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T10:11:32Z\nShowing lines 230-290 of 434\n230:     units: Sequence[HistoryUnit],\n231:     recent_turns: int = RECENT_TURN_COUNT,\n232: ) -> Tier0Selection:\n233:     \"\"\"Build the Tier 0 lock set for a history.\n234: \n235:     The lock covers every user message, the last six turns, every approval and\n236:     every decision, and the final result of every tool chain.\n237:     \"\"\"\n238:     user_ids = frozenset(unit.unit_id for unit in units if unit.kind is UnitKind.USER)\n239:     approval_ids = frozenset(unit.unit_id for unit in units if unit.kind is UnitKind.APPROVAL)\n240:     decision_ids = frozenset(unit.unit_id for unit in units if unit.kind is UnitKind.DECISION)\n241:     finals = tool_chain_final_ids(units)\n242:     recent = recent_turn_ids(units, recent_turns)\n243: \n244:     return Tier0Selection(\n245:         locked_ids=frozenset(user_ids | approval_ids | decision_ids | finals | recent),\n246:         user_ids=user_ids,\n247:         approval_ids=approval_ids,\n248:         decision_ids=decision_ids,\n249:         tool_chain_final_ids=finals,\n250:         recent_turn_ids=recent,\n251:     )\n252: \n253: \n254: # =============================================================================\n255: # Tier 1: Jev calibrated probability gate\n256: # =============================================================================\n257: \n258: TIER1_QUESTIONS: dict[str, str] = {\n259:     \"needed\": \"Is this unit needed to continue the current task?\",\n260:     \"user_fact\": \"Does this unit contain a fact that the user supplied?\",\n261:     \"bulk\": \"Is this unit raw bulk output, such as a log, a listing, or a large dump?\",\n262: }\n263: \n264: \n265: def decide_verdict(probabilities: Mapping[str, float]) -> Verdict:\n266:     \"\"\"Map three calibrated probabilities to one verdict.\n267: \n268:     A missing `needed` score means the gate cannot judge the unit, so the unit\n269:     stays. The retain branch runs first, then the stub branch, then the drop\n270:     branch.\n271:     \"\"\"\n272:     needed = probabilities.get(\"needed\")\n273:     if needed is None:\n274:         return Verdict.RETAIN\n275:     user_fact = probabilities.get(\"user_fact\", 0.0)\n276:     bulk = probabilities.get(\"bulk\", 0.0)\n277: \n278:     if needed >= NEEDED_RETAIN_THRESHOLD or user_fact >= USER_FACT_RETAIN_THRESHOLD:\n279:         return Verdict.RETAIN\n280:     if bulk >= BULK_STUB_THRESHOLD:\n281:         return Verdict.STUB\n282:     if needed < DROP_BELOW_THRESHOLD:\n283:         return Verdict.DROP\n284:     return Verdict.RETAIN\n285: \n286: \n287: @dataclass(frozen=True)\n288: class UnitScore:\n289:     \"\"\"Tier 1 score and unlocked verdict for one unit.\"\"\"\n290: "}
{"step_index":328,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:11:32Z","input_tokens":2401,"cache_read_tokens":86950,"output_tokens":536,"thinking":"The code identifies user IDs from the units, calculates recent turn IDs, and constructs a set of locked IDs by combining user IDs with other categories. Initial thought was the locking mechanism considered user messages. However, tier 0 locks every user message forever, which means every user ID is already locked.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":100,"Path":"/home/nasbombz/Documents/Projects/vigil-agent/backend/src/modules/runs/core_context.py","StartLine":1}}]}
{"step_index":329,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:11:40Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T10:11:40Z\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T10:11:40Z\nShowing lines 1-100 of 246\n1: \"\"\"Core agent context: embedded Karpathy guidelines, streamlined brainstorming, and skill catalog.\n2: \n3: The Vigil agent's engineering philosophy (Karpathy's 4 coding principles) and a\n4: streamlined, proportionate Brainstorming & Design-Before-Code discipline live\n5: directly in the core system prompt rather than as external loadable skills.\n6: Specialized domain skills (high-end visual design, coding/frontend engineering,\n7: and scientific literature research) are cataloged here and loaded on demand via\n8: ``skill_load``. The ``proactivity`` skill is the exception: it loads into\n9: context during initialisation so every run starts proactive.\n10: \"\"\"\n11: from __future__ import annotations\n12: \n13: from pathlib import Path\n14: from typing import Sequence\n15: \n16: DEFAULT_BUNDLED_SKILLS: tuple[str, ...] = (\n17:     \"high-end-visual-design\",\n18:     \"emil-design-eng\",\n19:     \"design-taste-frontend\",\n20:     \"literature-search-arxiv\",\n21:     \"literature-search-openalex\",\n22:     \"plus-ultra\",\n23:     \"ultrawork\",\n24:     \"marketing-psychology\",\n25:     \"proactivity\",\n26: )\n27: \n28: CORE_PHILOSOPHY_AND_BRAINSTORMING = \"\"\"## Core Engineering Philosophy (Karpathy Guidelines)\n29: \n30: Tradeoff bias: **Easier to ask than to rewrite. Simpler is better than clever. Surgical beats comprehensive. Verified beats assumed.**\n31: \n32: ### 1. Think Before Coding\n33: Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.\n34: - State your assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask or verify first.\n35: - If multiple interpretations exist, present them — don't pick silently.\n36: - Proactively ask clarifying follow-up questions when requirements are underspecified.\n37: - If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted.\n38: - Stop and clarify what is unclear rather than guessing across architectural boundaries.\n39: - Treat greetings and casual check-ins as conversational turn boundaries. Do not execute tools on a simple greeting.\n40: \n41: ### 2. Simplicity First\n42: Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.\n43: - No features beyond what was asked.\n44: - No abstractions for single-use code.\n45: - No \"flexibility\" or \"configurability\" that wasn't requested.\n46: - No speculative error handling for impossible scenarios.\n47: - If 200 lines could be 50, write 50. Always ask: *\"Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?\"*\n48: \n49: ### 3. Surgical Changes\n50: Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.\n51: - When editing existing code, don't \"improve\" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.\n52: - Don't refactor things that aren't broken.\n53: - Match existing project style, even if you'd do it differently.\n54: - If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it — don't delete it without asking.\n55: - Remove imports, variables, or functions that **your own** changes made unused. Every changed line must trace directly to the user's request.\n56: \n57: ### 4. Goal-Driven Execution\n58: Define success criteria. Loop until verified.\n59: - Transform imperative tasks into verifiable goals before or while executing:\n60:   - *\"Add validation\"* → Write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass.\n61:   - *\"Fix the bug\"* → Reproduce it first, then fix and verify the reproduction passes.\n62:   - *\"Build or refactor X\"* → Verify the build/tests pass and the output works before finishing.\n63: - For multi-step tasks, state a brief plan with a concrete verification check for each step:\n64:   ```\n65:   1. [Step] → verify: [check]\n66:   2. [Step] → verify: [check]\n67:   ```\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## Streamlined Brainstorming & Design-Before-Code Discipline\n72: \n73: Proportionate design thinking before implementation — without bureaucratic bloat:\n74: \n75: 1. **Explore Context First**: Before scaffolding or modifying files, inspect the existing workspace/files (`read`, `bash`) to understand current structure, conventions, and constraints.\n76: 2. **Proportionate Depth (Classify the Task)**:\n77:    - **Clear & Bounded Tasks** (bug fixes, small scripts, well-specified features): Do not stall with unnecessary questions. State a concise 2–4 line summary of your intended approach and verification check, then build and verify directly.\n78:    - **Creative / UI / Architectural Builds** (new apps, portfolios, landing pages, multi-component systems):\n79:      - If critical requirements are genuinely ambiguous and divergent, use `ask_user` with **one** focused question (preferring concrete choices) rather than a multi-question dump.\n80:      - When requirements are clear enough to proceed, formulate a crisp **Design Read & Architecture Brief** (aesthetic direction, layout/component structure, data flow, and verification plan) before writing code.\n81: 3. **Isolation & YAGNI**: Decompose complex builds into focused, testable units with clear boundaries. Strip out every unrequested feature.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## Technical Communication & Writing Standards (ASD-STE100 & Emoji Guidelines)\n86: \n87: Follow the core principles of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English) and strict emoji restraint in all plans, explanations, comments, and documentation:\n88: \n89: ### 1. Emoji Restraint\n90: - Avoid emojis in all text, plans, code comments, and documentation.\n91: - Use emojis only when strictly necessary for technical context (such as terminal status markers or user-requested UI elements).\n92: - In UI code and frontends, use vector icons (such as `lucide-react`) instead of emojis.\n93: \n94: ### 2. Core ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English) Principles\n95: Apply these rules to produce clear, direct, and unambiguous technical writing:\n96: \n97: 1. **Sentence Length**:\n98:    - Limit procedural sentences (instructions, actions, commands) to a maximum of 20 words.\n99:    - Limit descriptive sentences (explanations, specifications) to a maximum of 25 words.\n100: 2. **One Thought per Sentence**:"}
