{"step_index":383,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:15:08Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T10:15:08Z\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T10:15:08Z\nShowing lines 1016-1040 of 1138\n1016:     toolName === 'read' ||\n1017:     toolName === 'read_document' ||\n1018:     toolName === 'write' ||\n1019:     toolName === 'edit'\n1020:   ) {\n1021:     return <FileOperationCard item={item} active={active} />;\n1022:   }\n1023:   if (toolName === 'deliver_file' || toolName === 'generate_document') {\n1024:     return <SandboxDocumentCard item={item} active={active} />;\n1025:   }\n1026:   if (\n1027:     toolName === 'web_search' ||\n1028:     toolName === 'web_fetch' ||\n1029:     toolName.startsWith('exa_') ||\n1030:     toolName.includes('search')\n1031:   ) {\n1032:     return <WebSearchCard item={item} active={active} />;\n1033:   }\n1034: \n1035:   return <GenericToolCard item={item} active={active} />;\n1036: }\n1037: \n1038: export function ThinkingCapsule({ active, steps = [], summary, defaultOpen }: Props) {\n1039:   const items = useMemo(() => buildTimeline(steps), [steps]);\n1040:   const [open, setOpen] = useState<boolean>(defaultOpen ?? active);"}
{"step_index":384,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:15:08Z","input_tokens":2593,"cache_read_tokens":115445,"output_tokens":2720,"thinking":"The `ThinkingCapsule` renders an `InlineApprovalCard` when an item requires approval, using the item's step payload. The lack of arguments in the payload suggests differing storage of approval-related data such as `tool_name`, `action`, `arguments`, or `approvalId` when approvals are needed. The process requires further examination of how approvals and associated actions are handled in the Hark Pro component.\n\n\nThe user's feedback highlights a critical architectural issue: the agent appears to be bypassing proper component utilization, opting for overlays instead, especially concerning the `ThinkingCapsule`. This is indicated by the discrepancy between the expected structured presentation of approval cards and the observed implementation of direct action pills and integrated cards in other scenarios. Examination of the `web/src/App.tsx` file may reveal the root cause for this overlay approach and its impact on component usage within the web interface.\n\n\nThe `HarkUnifiedApp` component within `HomeRoute` and `ChatRoute` appears to directly integrate action pills, bypassing the intended approval card rendering. This suggests that the workflow logic intended for `ThinkingCapsule` might be overridden or differently implemented in this specific context. Investigating how the `HarkProactivityPill` components are invoked and their relation to the overall approval process will reveal whether the intended component hierarchy is followed.\n\n\nThe code reveals `HarkProactivityPill` components are directly integrated within the chat interface, independent of the intended `ThinkingCapsule` or `InlineApprovalCard`. The `onVerbClick` and `onPillClick` handlers define actions that bypass the standard approval workflow, suggesting a deviation from the expected component hierarchy. This supports the hypothesis of direct action implementations replacing the structured presentation of approvals.\n\n\nThe observed behavior involves direct UI element integration, bypassing the intended component usage, specifically with hard-coded mock screens. The system displays static screenshots for components such as `HarkLiveBrowserCard` and `HarkEmailCard` instead of proper component rendering. The workflow logic shows all of this is hidden and only `MessageStream` is shown when messages exist.\n\n\nThe legacy `ThinkingCapsule` in `MessageStream` explains the old, unstyled approval capsules and broken input field. The input field's incorrect positioning due to misplaced properties such as `pointer-events-none fixed inset-x-0 bottom-28 md:bottom-10 md:pl-20` on `InputPill` caused it to be clipped or off-screen. The app was still showing the marketing landing page on the root path, or redirecting unauthenticated users to a pairing page. Now to resolve the compaction issues, the trigger thresholds must be adjusted in both the `RunLoop` and settings to 8,000 tokens with a 0.7 ratio to trigger at 5,600 tokens or when the message count exceeds 10 messages. The locking strategy for Tier 0 locks must also be adjusted to avoid locking every user message forever, which makes compaction impossible as conversations grow. Instead, it should only lock the recent conversation window.\n\n\nThe compaction strategy considers recent conversation turns, active approvals, decisions, and tool outputs as critical data to retain, while older messages require special handling. A fallback mechanism with heuristic scoring is implemented when external tools are unavailable, prioritizing locked units and employing token thresholds to determine the fate of older content. Tier 2 summarization leverages a Fast LLM to condense dropped messages into a concise summary block that is prepended to the retained recent turns, significantly reducing token usage.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"uv run --directory backend pytest backend/tests/unit/ -k \"compact\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Run compaction tests"}}]}
