{"step_index":471,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:23:08Z","input_tokens":2705,"cache_read_tokens":159125,"output_tokens":1903,"thinking":"Initial investigation reveals that `resolve_verdicts` is not directly invoked in the unit tests. Further inspection of its implementation within the compaction module shows that it applies a safety lock based on Tier 0 results. This suggests its purpose is to enforce safety constraints across different tiers.\n\n\nTier 0's lock on a unit now enforces that Tier 1's verdict is RETAIN, unless the unit is a tool result *outside* of recent turns and has an artifact ID; in that case, the verdict may be STUB. This could dramatically reduce output size for stale tool results by replacing verbose output with a stub pointer. The locking mechanism appears to be determined by tool chain final IDs within `locked_ids`, and the recent turn IDs are used to identify recent actions.\n\n\nThe current logic assumes Tier 0 verdicts lock Tier 1 to RETAIN, but it overlooks cases where old tool results might be appropriately marked for DROP or STUB by Tier 1. The previous logic forces historic tool outputs into RETAIN, potentially leading to unbounded history growth. Re-examining the `resolve_verdicts` function confirms it is the central point for safety lock enforcement and can allow older tool results to be stubbed or dropped, preventing history bloat.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"edit_file","args":{"Path":"/home/nasbombz/Documents/Projects/vigil-agent/backend/src/modules/compaction/tiers.py","Replacement":"def resolve_verdicts(\n    units: Sequence[HistoryUnit],\n    tier1: Tier1Result,\n    tier0: Tier0Selection,\n) -> dict[str, Verdict]:\n    \"\"\"Apply the Tier 0 lock to every Tier 1 verdict.\n\n    This function is the single place where the safety lock is enforced.\n    Approvals, decisions, and recent conversation turns are strictly locked.\n    Historical tool results and assistant progress messages outside the recent\n    turns are judged by Tier 1 to allow stubbing and summarization.\n    \"\"\"\n    resolved: dict[str, Verdict] = {}\n    for unit in units:\n        score = tier1.score_for(unit.unit_id)\n        if unit.kind in (UnitKind.APPROVAL, UnitKind.DECISION) or unit.unit_id in tier0.recent_turn_ids:\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN\n            continue\n        if unit.kind is UnitKind.USER:\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN\n            continue\n        if score is not None:\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = score.verdict\n        elif tier0.is_locked(unit.unit_id):\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN\n        else:\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN\n    return resolved","Target":"def resolve_verdicts(\n    units: Sequence[HistoryUnit],\n    tier1: Tier1Result,\n    tier0: Tier0Selection,\n) -> dict[str, Verdict]:\n    \"\"\"Apply the Tier 0 lock to every Tier 1 verdict.\n\n    This function is the single place where the safety lock is enforced. A\n    locked unit keeps the verdict RETAIN whatever Tier 1 says.\n    \"\"\"\n    resolved: dict[str, Verdict] = {}\n    for unit in units:\n        if tier0.is_locked(unit.unit_id):\n            resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN\n            continue\n        score = tier1.score_for(unit.unit_id)\n        resolved[unit.unit_id] = Verdict.RETAIN if score is None else score.verdict\n    return resolved"}}]}
