{"step_index":653,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-11T10:46:24Z","input_tokens":2613,"cache_read_tokens":252924,"output_tokens":2307,"thinking":"The `submit()` function calls `onSend(draft)`. This function is defined within the `HarkUnifiedApp` component and is passed as a prop to the `InputPill` component. The `InputPill` component then invokes the `chat.send(text)` method when triggered.\n\n\nThe `send` function in `chat.ts` checks for a `target` before sending, which is derived from `sessionIdRef.current`. The `HarkUnifiedApp` component manages `activeSessionId`, initially deriving it from URL parameters or existing sessions. If no session ID is available at app startup, `activeSessionId` becomes null, leading to `sessionIdRef.current` also becoming null within the `useAgentChat` hook. This null `target` then causes the `send` function to early return.\n\n\nThe `chat.send()` function immediately returns because the target session ID is null, preventing message delivery. Even if a session existed, the backend call could still fail due to authentication or missing device token, leading to an undefined configuration ID and an unhandled error state, where the error is not displayed to the user.\n\nTo correct this, if no session ID exists when `chat.send()` is called, a session must be created inline. The `handleSend` function will be updated to be asynchronous, creating a new session if one does not exist prior to sending the message.\n\n\nIf no session ID exists, the `handleSend` function will now create a new session before attempting to send the message. The `send` function in the chat module has been updated to accept an optional session ID, ensuring messages are sent with the correct target session. A new check now ensures the default configuration is loaded before attempting to retrieve its ID, preventing the function from prematurely exiting.\n\nThe authentication header requirement explains why the API request fails locally. The header is strictly required in the backend, and is missing locally.\n\n\nThe 422 errors suggest an authentication issue, specifically related to the missing bearer token. The code provides a fallback for \"development\" and \"test\" environments, automatically authenticating with a default local device. The dependency injection correctly handles this. The `get_or_create_default_device` function, will create a default device if one does not exist.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":42,"Path":"/home/nasbombz/Documents/Projects/vigil-agent/backend/src/modules/auth/dependencies.py","StartLine":15}}]}
